WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.530
Logging is production code, and production code has performance and reliability consequences.

2
00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:13.591
A log call can allocate memory, format strings, acquire locks, write to disk, send data to an agent, or block behind a slow sink.

3
00:00:13.591 --> 00:00:21.141
Synchronous logging is simple to reason about, but it can add latency or stall work if the destination is slow.

4
00:00:21.141 --> 00:00:28.314
Asynchronous logging can reduce direct latency, but it introduces queues, buffering, shutdown behavior, and backpressure decisions that must be

5
00:00:28.314 --> 00:00:28.692
designed.

6
00:00:28.692 --> 00:00:30.957
Repeated errors can create log storms.

7
00:00:30.957 --> 00:00:40.395
A tight loop that logs every failure, a noisy retry path, or many threads reporting the same condition can consume CPU, disk, network bandwidth, and

8
00:00:40.395 --> 00:00:41.150
operator attention.

9
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.170
Rate limiting, aggregation, and sampling may be appropriate.

10
00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.568
Thread safety matters in C++ logging libraries and wrappers.

11
00:00:47.568 --> 00:00:52.476
Shared sinks, formatter state, buffers, and locks need clear ownership and concurrency behavior.

12
00:00:52.476 --> 00:00:59.649
Lock contention in a hot path can become a performance problem even when each individual log call looks small.

13
00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:01.536
Plan for unavailable log sinks.

14
00:01:01.536 --> 00:01:09.464
The application should define whether to drop logs, buffer temporarily, apply backpressure, switch to a fallback destination, or fail closed for

15
00:01:09.464 --> 00:01:10.974
special audit-sensitive paths.

16
00:01:10.974 --> 00:01:13.617
The policy should be intentional and documented.

17
00:01:13.617 --> 00:01:16.260
Logging failures should not corrupt application state.

18
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:22.677
If a log write fails, the error-handling path should preserve invariants and avoid recursive logging loops.

19
00:01:22.677 --> 00:01:27.208
Monitoring should make logging health visible without causing another burst of events.

20
00:01:27.208 --> 00:01:31.738
Excessive logging can weaken availability and make important events harder to find.

21
00:01:31.738 --> 00:01:40.421
Safe logging balances detail, cost, and signal so operators receive useful information when the system is healthy and when it is under pressure.

22
00:01:40.421 --> 00:01:44.574
Performance review should include both the call site and the pipeline.

23
00:01:44.574 --> 00:01:52.879
Formatting work in a hot path, queue growth in an async logger, collector delays, and storage quotas can all affect production behavior.

24
00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:54.767
Concurrency review should include shutdown.

25
00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:59.297
Threads may still be writing events while buffers flush or sinks close.

26
00:01:59.297 --> 00:02:04.960
A safe design defines ordering, ownership, and what happens to queued records during controlled shutdown.
